<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<databaseChangeLog
    xmlns="http://www.liquibase.org/xml/ns/dbchangelog"
    xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance"
    xsi:schemaLocation="http://www.liquibase.org/xml/ns/dbchangelog
        http://www.liquibase.org/xml/ns/dbchangelog/dbchangelog-latest.xsd">

    <!-- Statistics page load time (issue #1022).

         Every aggregate behind the page filters log_entry on the expression

             COALESCE(timestamp_forwarding, timestamp_request, timestamp_response)

         and none of the existing indexes covers it, so each one fell back to a
         sequential scan. Measured against a production snapshot (689k log_entry
         rows, 2.4M usage_tokens), one page load cost 6.5 s of database time on
         the production host, 3.4 s of it in findTotals alone.

         An index on that expression does not fix the default view: the 30-day
         preset selects roughly 60 percent of the table, where a sequential scan
         is the cheaper plan and the planner rightly picks it. Counting 400k+
         rows into 120 buckets is O(n) work that only pre-aggregation removes.

         Hence the rollup below. At its finest grain the whole history collapses
         to about 12.5k rows (3.4 MB), a factor of 55, and every aggregate
         becomes a scan of that instead of the base table. Measured on the same
         snapshot the eight page queries went from 1769 ms to 22 ms locally, and
         the merged reader (rollup plus a live tail) answers the 30-day totals in
         51 ms against 504 ms live.

         The expression index is still created: it is what makes the live tail
         and the short presets (last hour, today) cheap, which is exactly the
         selectivity range where an index does win.

         Freshness: the view holds only *complete* hours, so a partial head hour
         and everything newer than the last refresh are read from log_entry and
         merged on top. Correctness therefore never depends on the refresh having
         run - a stale or lagging view only shifts work back to the base table.
         RequestLogStatsRefreshService refreshes it hourly. -->

    <!-- log_entry_cost calls logos_price_usage, which reaches for `providers`
         without a schema. CREATE and REFRESH MATERIALIZED VIEW run with a
         restricted search_path, so any rollup over that view fails with
         "relation providers does not exist" until the functions carry their own
         search_path. Pinning it is the fix and is worth having on its own: a
         function whose meaning depends on the caller's search_path is a latent
         hazard well beyond this view. -->
    <changeSet id="035-pin-pricing-function-search-path" author="logos">
        <sql splitStatements="false"><![CDATA[
            DO $$
            DECLARE fn record;
            BEGIN
                FOR fn IN
                    SELECT p.oid::regprocedure AS signature
                    FROM pg_proc p
                    JOIN pg_namespace n ON n.oid = p.pronamespace
                    WHERE n.nspname = 'public'
                      AND p.proname LIKE 'logos\_%'
                LOOP
                    EXECUTE format('ALTER FUNCTION %s SET search_path = public, pg_catalog',
                                   fn.signature);
                END LOOP;
            END $$;
        ]]></sql>
        <rollback>
            <sql splitStatements="false"><![CDATA[
                DO $$
                DECLARE fn record;
                BEGIN
                    FOR fn IN
                        SELECT p.oid::regprocedure AS signature
                        FROM pg_proc p
                        JOIN pg_namespace n ON n.oid = p.pronamespace
                        WHERE n.nspname = 'public'
                          AND p.proname LIKE 'logos\_%'
                    LOOP
                        EXECUTE format('ALTER FUNCTION %s RESET search_path', fn.signature);
                    END LOOP;
                END $$;
            ]]></sql>
        </rollback>
    </changeSet>

    <!-- Serves the live tail above the rollup watermark and the short presets.
         Outside a transaction so CONCURRENTLY can build without locking
         log_entry against the logos-orchestrator, which keeps writing to this
         table while logos-webservice is redeployed (same rationale as
         012/014/027/028). -->
    <changeSet id="035-log-entry-effective-ts-index" author="logos" runInTransaction="false">
        <preConditions onFail="MARK_RAN">
            <not><indexExists tableName="log_entry" indexName="idx_log_entry_effective_ts"/></not>
        </preConditions>
        <sql>
            CREATE INDEX CONCURRENTLY idx_log_entry_effective_ts
                ON log_entry ((COALESCE(timestamp_forwarding, timestamp_request, timestamp_response)));
        </sql>
        <rollback>
            <sql>DROP INDEX CONCURRENTLY IF EXISTS idx_log_entry_effective_ts;</sql>
        </rollback>
    </changeSet>

    <!-- The rollup itself.

         Grain is (hour, model, provider, team, user, status, cold start): every
         dimension the page groups or filters by, so one view answers the totals,
         the status counts, the model breakdown, both time series and the
         cold/warm split. provider_id and model_id stay as ids rather than baked
         privacy levels or names, so the reader joins providers/models live and a
         renamed model or a changed privacy level needs no refresh to show up.

         Averages are stored as sum plus count, never as an average: averaging
         pre-averaged hours would weight a quiet hour like a busy one.

         Only complete hours are included (now() is re-evaluated on every
         refresh), which is what lets the reader treat max(bucket_hour) + 1 hour
         as a watermark it can trust and read anything above it live.

         Created WITH DATA: an unpopulated materialized view raises on SELECT
         rather than returning nothing, so the reader could not fall back. The
         one-off build cost is roughly a minute on production. -->
    <changeSet id="035-log-entry-hourly-stats" author="logos">
        <preConditions onFail="MARK_RAN">
            <sqlCheck expectedResult="0">
                SELECT COUNT(*) FROM pg_matviews WHERE matviewname = 'log_entry_hourly_stats'
            </sqlCheck>
        </preConditions>
        <sql splitStatements="false"><![CDATA[
            CREATE MATERIALIZED VIEW log_entry_hourly_stats AS
            SELECT
                date_trunc('hour', COALESCE(le.timestamp_forwarding,
                                            le.timestamp_request,
                                            le.timestamp_response))          AS bucket_hour,
                le.model_id,
                le.provider_id,
                le.team_id,
                le.user_id,
                le.result_status,
                COALESCE(le.was_cold_start, FALSE)                           AS was_cold_start,
                COUNT(*)                                                     AS requests,
                SUM(CASE WHEN le.timestamp_request IS NOT NULL
                          AND le.timestamp_forwarding IS NOT NULL
                         THEN EXTRACT(EPOCH FROM (le.timestamp_forwarding - le.timestamp_request))
                    END)                                                     AS queue_seconds_sum,
                COUNT(*) FILTER (WHERE le.timestamp_request IS NOT NULL
                                   AND le.timestamp_forwarding IS NOT NULL)  AS queue_seconds_count,
                SUM(CASE WHEN le.timestamp_forwarding IS NOT NULL
                          AND le.timestamp_response IS NOT NULL
                         THEN EXTRACT(EPOCH FROM (le.timestamp_response - le.timestamp_forwarding))
                    END)                                                     AS run_seconds_sum,
                COUNT(*) FILTER (WHERE le.timestamp_forwarding IS NOT NULL
                                   AND le.timestamp_response IS NOT NULL)    AS run_seconds_count,
                COUNT(*) FILTER (WHERE le.result_status IS DISTINCT FROM 'success'
                                    OR (le.error_message IS NOT NULL
                                        AND le.error_message <> ''))         AS error_count,
                COALESCE(SUM(tok.total_tokens), 0)::bigint                   AS total_tokens,
                COALESCE(SUM(lec.cost_micro_cents), 0)::bigint               AS cost_micro_cents
            FROM log_entry le
            LEFT JOIN LATERAL (
                SELECT SUM(ut.token_count) AS total_tokens
                FROM usage_tokens ut
                JOIN token_types tt ON tt.id = ut.type_id
                WHERE ut.log_entry_id = le.id
                  AND tt.name = 'total_tokens'
            ) tok ON TRUE
            LEFT JOIN log_entry_cost lec ON lec.log_entry_id = le.id
            WHERE COALESCE(le.timestamp_forwarding, le.timestamp_request, le.timestamp_response)
                  < date_trunc('hour', now())
            GROUP BY 1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 7;
        ]]></sql>
        <!-- REFRESH MATERIALIZED VIEW CONCURRENTLY requires a unique index over
             the grain; it is also what keeps the grain honest. -->
        <sql>
            CREATE UNIQUE INDEX ux_log_entry_hourly_stats_grain
                ON log_entry_hourly_stats (bucket_hour, model_id, provider_id, team_id,
                                           user_id, result_status, was_cold_start);
        </sql>
        <sql>
            CREATE INDEX idx_log_entry_hourly_stats_bucket
                ON log_entry_hourly_stats (bucket_hour);
        </sql>
        <!-- The single split point between "read the rollup" and "read
             log_entry", shared by every statistics aggregate.

             It is a function and not five copies of the same CTE on purpose: if
             two aggregates disagreed by one hour about where the rollup ends,
             one would double-count the overlap and another would drop a gap, and
             the page would contradict itself in a way no single query looks
             wrong for.

               mv_lo  first whole hour at or after p_start - a range starting at
                      03:43 must not pull in all of the 03:00 bucket, so that
                      partial head hour stays with the live side.
               mv_hi  exclusive upper bound: the last hour the range covers,
                      capped at the rollup watermark (max bucket + 1h). Floored
                      at mv_lo so that an empty or lagging rollup yields an empty
                      window rather than an inverted one - which is what makes
                      "live covers everything below mv_lo or at/above mv_hi"
                      exactly a partition, with no overlap and no gap.

             p_use_rollup = false collapses the window to empty and sends the
             whole query to log_entry; that is how sub-hour buckets are served. -->
        <sql splitStatements="false"><![CDATA[
            CREATE OR REPLACE FUNCTION logos_stats_rollup_window(
                p_start timestamptz,
                p_end timestamptz,
                p_use_rollup boolean
            )
            RETURNS TABLE (mv_lo timestamptz, mv_hi timestamptz)
            LANGUAGE sql
            STABLE
            SET search_path = public, pg_catalog
            AS $$
                WITH lo AS (
                    SELECT CASE WHEN p_start = date_trunc('hour', p_start)
                                THEN p_start
                                ELSE date_trunc('hour', p_start) + interval '1 hour'
                           END AS mv_lo
                )
                SELECT lo.mv_lo,
                       CASE WHEN p_use_rollup THEN
                           GREATEST(
                               LEAST(
                                   date_trunc('hour', p_end + interval '1 microsecond'),
                                   (SELECT COALESCE(max(bucket_hour) + interval '1 hour',
                                                    '-infinity'::timestamptz)
                                      FROM log_entry_hourly_stats)
                               ),
                               lo.mv_lo)
                       ELSE lo.mv_lo END
                FROM lo;
            $$;
        ]]></sql>
        <rollback>
            <sql>DROP FUNCTION IF EXISTS logos_stats_rollup_window(timestamptz, timestamptz, boolean);</sql>
            <sql>DROP MATERIALIZED VIEW IF EXISTS log_entry_hourly_stats;</sql>
        </rollback>
    </changeSet>

</databaseChangeLog>
